.TH BTKEYFS 4
.SH NAME
btkeyfs \- Bluetooth bonds, served
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bt/btkeyfs
[
.BI -m " mountpoint"
] [
.BI -f " keyfile"
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Btkeyfs
keeps the long term keys that pairing produces, and serves them.  It
is what
.IR btfs (4)
is given with
.B -K
instead of a key file of its own.
.PP
A bond is not part of a controller.  It outlives the
.I btfs
that made it, it belongs to a pair of devices rather than to the radio
the pairing happened to go through, and it is the one thing in the
stack that must not be readable by everything that can read a radio.
So it is kept by a program of its own, reached the way everything else
here is reached: by opening, reading and writing files.
.PP
The name space, by default at
.BR /net/btkeys .
It is a sibling of
.B /net/bt
and not a directory inside it, because
.I btfs
mounts on
.B /net/bt
with
.BR MREPL :
anything mounted underneath first would go when it did.
.TP
.B bonds
One line per bond, in the format
.IR bt (2)
prints and parses \- the peer, its address type, the keys, and
.BI on " addr" ,
the controller the bond was made on.  It is the same text a
.B btfs
.B bonds
file shows and the same text a key file holds, because it is the same
code that writes it.
.TP
.B ctl
.B bond
followed by such a line adds it, replacing any bond for the same peer
on the same controller.  A line with no long term key in it is
refused: a peer that looked paired and could not encrypt would be
worse than one that has to pair again.
.IP
.BI forget " addr"
removes every bond for that peer;
.BI "forget " addr " on " local
removes only the one made on that controller, since the same peer may
be bonded to two radios and forgetting it on one is not forgetting it
on the other.
.IP
.B sync
writes the store out now.  It is not usually needed: every change is
written as it is made.
.PP
Both files are mode 0600 and belong to the user who started
.IR btkeyfs .
That is the point of the program: the radio being readable does not
make the key readable.
.PP
.B -f
names the file the store is kept in, and without it the bonds last
only as long as the program.  The file holds exactly what
.B bonds
reads, so a key file written by
.I btfs
.B -k
is a store
.I btkeyfs
can be started on, and the other way round.
.SH EXAMPLE
Serve the bonds, and run the stack against them:
.IP
.EX
bt/btkeyfs -f /adm/btkeys
bt/btfs -K /net/btkeys /dev/eia1!115200
.EE
.PP
Forget a peer on one radio:
.IP
.EX
echo forget 060504030201 on 001122334455 > /net/btkeys/ctl
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/bt/btkeyfs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR btfs (4),
.IR bt (2)
.SH BUGS
Nothing authenticates a client: whoever can walk to the mount point
can read every key in it.  The permissions on the files are the whole
of the protection, so where the service is mounted is the decision
that matters.
